add(334,'planar-jacobian','Extracting the planar body Jacobian','Which matrix maps [q1dot,q2dot] to the frame3 x/y tip velocity?', ['[[L1 sinθ2,0],[L2+L1 cosθ2,L2]]','[[L1 cosθ2,0],[L2+L1 sinθ2,L2]]','[[L1 sinθ2,L2],[L1 cosθ2,0]]','[[L1 sinθ2,L2+L1 cosθ2],[0,L2]]'],0,'Collecting coefficients of the two joint rates produces the displayed2×2 matrix.', ['The columns are the coefficients of each joint rate.','This interchanges sine and cosine.','This places the second-joint contribution in x rather than y.','This is the transpose, which is used for forces rather than velocity mapping.'],typ='derivation',steps=['Collect q1dot and q2dot in the x equation.','Repeat for the y equation.','Place each output’s coefficients in its row.'],formulas=[jac])
add(334,'planar-projection','Why the task Jacobian has two rows','Why can the worked position Jacobian use two rows even though the preceding velocity vector has three components?', ['The planar z velocity is identically zero, so the x/y task drops that zero row','The angular velocity was combined with linear z velocity into one scalar','A two-joint arm necessarily moves in three independent Cartesian directions','The third row was removed because its entries depend on joint rates'],0,'The slide projects the translational velocity onto the active planar x/y coordinates; it is not claiming a full six-component twist map.', ['This makes the dimensions consistent.','Linear and angular components were not combined.','The planar arm has only two position-task coordinates.','Jacobian entries depend on configuration, and the omitted row is zero.'],typ='conceptual',assumptions=['Interpret the displayed2×2 matrices as planar position Jacobians; the written three-component vector includes a zero z entry.'])
add(334,'jacobian-columns','Interpreting the second body-Jacobian column','With q1dot=0 and q2dot=1 rad/s, what velocity does the second column encode?', ['(0,L2) m/s in the planar frame3 coordinates','(L2,0) m/s in frame3','(L1 sinθ2,L2+L1 cosθ2) m/s in frame3','(0,L1+L2) m/s for every configuration'],0,'The second joint rotates the tip about the elbow, producing motion perpendicular to the second link.', ['This is the second column times the unit rate.','This places velocity along the second link rather than tangentially.','This is the first column.','The first-link length does not contribute when the first joint is fixed.'],typ='diagram',assumptions=['Lengths are measured in meters and the given unit angular rate is1 rad/s.'])
add([333,334],'jacobian-frame','Changing the Jacobian expression frame','For the same tip point, how are the two planar Jacobians related?', ['J⁰=R(θ1+θ2)J³','J⁰=J³R(θ1+θ2)','J⁰=(J³)ᵀ','J⁰=(J³)⁻¹ whenever θ1 changes'],0,'Changing the output-coordinate basis multiplies the velocity map on the left.', ['R acts on the output velocity.','Right multiplication would mix the joint-coordinate inputs instead.','Transpose is the force dual, not a frame rotation.','Inversion reverses the velocity map and need not exist.'],typ='derivation',formulas=[jac])
add(335,'singularity-meaning','Meaning of singularity','For this two-dimensional task, what does rank loss mean instantaneously?', ['Some Cartesian velocity directions cannot be produced by finite joint rates','All joint positions become undefined','Every nonzero joint rate necessarily produces zero tip velocity','The end effector cannot reach any nearby pose through any finite motion sequence'],0,'Rank loss reduces the instantaneous image of J; it does not erase all joint motion or imply global immobility.', ['This is the local velocity-space statement.','Joint coordinates can remain well-defined.','There can still be one available velocity direction.','Instantaneous restrictions do not prove all finite paths impossible.'],typ='conceptual')
add([335,349],'determinant-scope','Scope of the determinant test','Which singularity test is properly scoped?', ['Use det J=0 for a square task Jacobian; use rank for a general rectangular Jacobian','Every6×n Jacobian has a determinant whose zero set gives singularity','A nonzero trace proves a Jacobian is nonsingular','Count the number of links instead of inspecting the velocity map'],0,'The determinant shorthand applies to square matrices; rank expresses the more general loss of task directions.', ['This preserves the example and states its limit.','Rectangular matrices do not have the ordinary determinant used here.','For example diag(1,0) has nonzero trace but is singular.','The actual task map determines local rank.'],typ='conceptual',assumptions=['The lecture’s determinant example is a square2×2 planar position map.'])
add(336,'singularity-conditions','Stretched and folded configurations','For positive nonzero L1 and L2, which θ2 values make the planar Jacobian singular?', ['θ2=kπ for integer k','θ2=π/2+kπ for integer k','Only θ1=0, regardless of θ2','Only L1=L2, regardless of the joint angles'],0,'det J=L1L2 sinθ2 vanishes when the two links are collinear.', ['This includes the displayed0° and180° cases.','These angles maximize the sine magnitude.','The determinant is independent of θ1.','Equal lengths alone do not cause rank loss.'],typ='derivation',formulas=[det])
add(336,'singular-direction','Motion direction in the straight-arm diagram','At θ1=0 and θ2=0 with positive link lengths, which instantaneous planar tip velocity direction remains possible?', ['Along base Y, perpendicular to the straight arm','Along base X, parallel to the straight arm','Any direction because both joints can rotate','No direction because the entire Jacobian is zero'],0,'The base Jacobian has zero first row and second row[L1+L2,L2], so its image is the Y direction.', ['Both joint columns are vertical.','There is no first-order radial extension at this instant.','The columns are dependent despite two joints.','Its nonzero second row still permits tangential motion.'],typ='diagram',steps=['Substitute sin0=0 and cos0=1.','J⁰=[[0,0],[L1+L2,L2]].','Every output has vx=0; vy can be nonzero.'],formulas=[jac])
add(336,'folded-rank','Equal-link folded special case','At L1=L2=L>0 and θ2=π, what is the body position Jacobian and its rank?', ['[[0,0],[0,L]], rank1','[[0,0],[0,0]], rank0','[[L,0],[0,L]], rank2','[[0,L],[L,0]], rank2'],0,'The first column vanishes when equal links fold to the base, but moving the elbow joint alone still gives a nonzero tangential tip velocity.', ['This substitutes sinπ=0 and cosπ=−1.','It incorrectly drops the second-joint column.','The collinear geometry is not full rank.','The nonzero off-diagonal entries do not follow from the formula.'],typ='derivation',formulas=[jac])
add(337,'inverse-example','Inverse Jacobian for a Cartesian command','For L1=L2=1 m, θ1=0°, θ2=90° and commanded(vx,vy)=(1,0) m/s, which joint rates solve the task?', ['(0,−1) rad/s','(1,0) rad/s','(−1,1) rad/s','(1,−1) rad/s'],0,'J⁰=[[-1,−1],[1,0]], so vy=q1dot=0 and vx=−q2dot=1.', ['Both Cartesian equations are satisfied.','This also gives an unwanted Y velocity.','This gives zero X velocity.','This gives zero X velocity and nonzero Y velocity.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Evaluate J⁰ at the stated angles.','From the second row, q1dot=0.','From the first row, q2dot=−1.'],formulas=[inv])
add(337,'near-singular-growth','Rate growth on a horizontal approach','For L1=L2=1 m, θ1=−θ2/2 and vx=1 m/s, vy=0, what happens as θ2 approaches0 through nonzero positive values?', ['q1dot grows positive without bound and q2dot grows negative without bound in the ideal inverse model','Both rates approach0 because the arm is straightening','Both rates approach+1 rad/s','q1dot remains0 while q2dot approaches−1 rad/s'],0,'cos(θ2/2) tends to1 while sinθ2 tends to0; q1dot is their positive ratio and q2dot is minus twice that ratio.', ['The signs and growth follow the two displayed inverse formulas.','The requested radial velocity does not shrink.','The denominator cannot be ignored.','That finite pair belongs to a different configuration and command geometry.'],typ='derivation',diff='hard',steps=['Set cosθ1=cos(θ1+θ2)=cos(θ2/2).','q1dot=cos(θ2/2)/sinθ2.','q2dot=−2cos(θ2/2)/sinθ2.','As θ2→0+, the common positive denominator tends to0.'],formulas=[inv],assumptions=['This is a limit of the ideal inverse calculation, not a physically achievable infinite actuator speed.'])
add(337,'rate-growth-qualification','Why not every near-singular command diverges','Keep θ1=π/2, let θ2 approach0 through nonzero values, and command(vx,vy)=(1,0) m/s; what do the same inverse formulas give?', ['q1dot=−1/L1 and q2dot=+1/L1, finite rates','Both joint rates must diverge for every θ1','q1dot=0 and q2dot=0','q1dot=+1/L2 and q2dot=−1/L2'],0,'cos(π/2+θ2)=−sinθ2 and cos(π/2)=0 cancel the apparent singular denominator for this tangential command.', ['The algebra shows why the command direction matters.','The source example’s growth must not be universalized to every direction.','Zero rates would not produce the commanded motion.','These signs and the L2 dependence do not match the substitution.'],typ='derivation',diff='hard',steps=['Use cosθ1=0.','Use cos(θ1+θ2)=−sinθ2.','Substitute into both nonzero-sinθ2 inverse expressions.','Obtain the finite pair(−1/L1,+1/L1).'],formulas=[inv],assumptions=['vx=1 m/s; sinθ2≠0 during the approach; L1 andL2 are positive lengths.'],conflict='The slide describes both joint rates diverging on its illustrated straightening path; that statement is not valid for every command direction or every approach.')
add(338,'static-method','Static-equilibrium procedure','Which sequence follows the lecture’s statics method?', ['Treat joints as locked → write each link’s force/moment balance → project the required effort onto each joint axis','Project joint efforts → integrate acceleration → replace all link balances with velocity propagation','Write only force balance → ignore moments → infer effort from link length alone','Unlock joints → prescribe arbitrary accelerations → call the resulting motion static equilibrium'],0,'Static equilibrium is determined from force and moment balance, then the actuator component along the joint freedom is extracted.', ['This matches the three steps.','Acceleration integration is not this static procedure.','Moment balance is essential for torque.','Nonzero arbitrary acceleration contradicts the stated static condition.'],typ='sequence',diff='easy')
add(339,'gravity-exclusion','Assumptions of the static example','Which load is explicitly omitted from the lecture’s link-balance derivation?', ['Gravity acting on the links','The specified end-effector load','The moments transmitted between adjacent links','All reaction forces at the joints'],0,'The source excludes link weight while retaining the end load and transmitted force/moment balances.', ['This is the stated simplifying assumption.','The end load is the quantity being supported.','Moments are propagated in the following derivation.','The joint reactions are what the link balances solve.'],typ='definition',diff='easy')
add(339,'force-sign','Meaning of the neighboring-link force','Under the slide’s definition of f_i, which statement explains the minus sign on f_(i+1) in link i’s balance?', ['f_(i+1) is defined as link i’s action on link i+1, so the force on link i is its opposite','Both symbols are already forces on link i in the same direction','The minus sign rotates the vector into frame i','The minus sign accounts for gravity on link i'],0,'The neighboring-link action/reaction pair has opposite directions; coordinate conversion is a separate operation.', ['This matches the stated definition and equilibrium sign.','That interpretation would not match the defined recipient of f_(i+1).','Rotation needs a rotation matrix.','Gravity is explicitly excluded.'],typ='conceptual')
add(340,'inward-propagation','Direction of static-load propagation','Given an end-effector force and moment, which direction does the shown statics recursion proceed?', ['From the hand toward the base','From the base toward the hand using joint rates','From the middle link outward in both directions without boundary data','From the force vector to a position inverse without link geometry'],0,'The distal load supplies boundary data; each preceding link adds the transmitted and offset moment.', ['This is the stated downward recursion.','That is the velocity-propagation direction, not the prescribed-load statics recursion.','A boundary load and geometry are needed.','The recursion uses rotations and moment arms, not an inverse position solve.'],typ='definition',diff='easy')
add(340,'moment-arm','Why moment propagation needs a cross product','What does p_(i+1)×f_(i+1) represent in moment balance about origin i?', ['The moment of the downstream force due to its offset from origin i','The downstream force magnitude multiplied by joint angle','A rotation that changes only the force coordinates','The actuator torque projected onto z before computing the total moment'],0,'A force at an offset contributes the vector moment r×f about the chosen origin.', ['This is the lever-arm contribution.','Angle is not the required displacement vector.','A cross product here creates a moment, not a coordinate rotation.','Projection comes after the full moment is computed.'],typ='conceptual')
