stat=form('f_i=R_i,i+1 f_(i+1); n_i=R_i,i+1 n_(i+1)+p_i,i+1×f_i',{'f_i,f_(i+1)':'transmitted force in each link frame','n_i,n_(i+1)':'transmitted moment about each origin','R_i,i+1':'rotation from downstream coordinates into frame i','p_i,i+1':'vector from origin i to origin i+1, expressed in frame i'},{'f_i,f_(i+1)':'N','n_i,n_(i+1)':'N·m','p_i,i+1':'m','R_i,i+1':'dimensionless'},['Static equilibrium; no link gravity or other distributed loads in this example.'])
force2r=form('τ1=L1 sinθ2 f_x+(L2+L1 cosθ2)f_y; τ2=L2 f_y',{'τ1,τ2':'joint efforts required to apply the specified end force','f_x,f_y':'force components exerted by the hand, expressed in frame3','L1,L2':'link lengths','θ2':'elbow angle'},{'τ1,τ2':'N·m','f_x,f_y':'N','L1,L2':'m','θ2':'rad'},['No applied terminal couple; link gravity omitted; positive efforts increase the corresponding angles.'])
virtual=form('δW=Fᵀδχ=τᵀδq; δχ=Jδq; τ=JᵀF',{'δW':'virtual work','F':'task wrench or force, matched to task displacement','δχ':'infinitesimal task displacement','τ':'generalized joint efforts','δq':'infinitesimal joint displacement','J':'task Jacobian'},{'δW':'J','force components':'N','moment components and revolute efforts':'N·m','translation and prismatic displacement':'m','rotation and revolute displacement':'rad','prismatic efforts':'N'},['Consistent frame, reference point and force sign; ideal virtual work; F is the wrench the manipulator is required to exert.'])
add(340,'cross-product-sign','Calculating the transmitted moment','With aligned frames, p=(0.4,0,0) m, downstream force f=(0,5,0) N and downstream moment n_next=(0,0,1) N·m, what is n_i?', ['(0,0,3) N·m','(0,0,−1) N·m','(0,0,2) N·m','(0,0,1.4) N·m'],0,'p×f contributes+2 N·m about z, which adds to the existing+1 N·m couple.', ['This includes both contributions with the right-hand sign.','This uses f×p instead of p×f.','This omits the transmitted couple.','This treats the moment-arm length itself as a moment.'],typ='numerical',steps=['p×f=(0,0,0.4×5)=(0,0,2).','Identity rotation leaves n_next unchanged.','Add(0,0,1)+(0,0,2).'],formulas=[stat])
add(340,'frame-before-sum','Coordinates before adding moments','A downstream moment is expressed in frame i+1; what must happen before adding it to p×f computed in frame i?', ['Rotate the downstream moment into frame i','Add its components directly because moments are frame-independent scalars','Rotate only p, leaving the moment in frame i+1','Replace the downstream moment with its magnitude'],0,'Vector components must use one basis before vector addition; moment magnitude alone loses direction.', ['This is the first term of the recursion.','A moment vector is not a scalar.','This still mixes the two component bases.','The moment’s direction matters to projection and balance.'],typ='conceptual')
add(340,'pure-couple','Origin shift of a pure couple','If the downstream force is zero but its moment is nonzero, how does a change of origin affect the propagated moment?', ['It adds no lever-arm term; only coordinate rotation remains','It adds p×n as an extra moment','It makes the moment zero at every upstream origin','It changes the couple magnitude in proportion to link length'],0,'The translation term is p×f, so a pure couple with f=0 is independent of origin, though its coordinates rotate.', ['This follows directly from the recursion.','The lever-arm cross product uses force, not moment.','A nonzero couple is still transmitted.','Changing the origin does not scale a pure couple.'],typ='derivation',formulas=[stat])
add(341,'joint-projection','Revolute and prismatic actuator efforts','Which ordered pair gives actuator effort for a revolute joint and a prismatic joint, respectively?', ['n·ẑ in N·m; f·ẑ in N','f·ẑ in N; n·ẑ in N·m','|n| in N·m; |f| in N for every joint axis','n×ẑ in N·m; f×ẑ in N'],0,'Each actuator supplies the scalar component conjugate to its one allowed motion: axis moment for rotation, axis force for translation.', ['Both projections and units match the slide.','This swaps the joint types.','The full magnitude is not generally the axis component.','Cross products produce vectors, not the required scalar projections.'],typ='match',diff='easy')
add(341,'joint-projection','Numerical projection onto a joint axis','In one common frame, a revolute joint axis is ẑ=(0,0,1) and the transmitted moment is n=(3,−4,12) N·m; what actuator torque follows?', ['12 N·m','13 N·m','−12 N·m','5 N·m'],0,'The actuator torque is the z component, not the full moment magnitude.', ['n·ẑ=12.','13 is the norm of n, which includes components constrained by the joint structure.','The stated positive axis gives the positive sign.','5 is the transverse moment magnitude, not the axial component.'],typ='numerical',steps=['τ=n·ẑ.','τ=3×0+(−4)×0+12×1.','τ=12 N·m.'],formulas=[form('τ=n·ẑ',{'τ':'revolute actuator torque','n':'moment in the same frame as the unit axis','ẑ':'positive unit joint axis'},{'τ,n':'N·m','ẑ':'dimensionless'})])
add(341,'effort-sign','Positive joint effort','What defines positive revolute joint torque in the lecture?', ['A moment tending to increase the chosen joint angle','A moment pointing upward in the room regardless of the joint convention','A moment with positive magnitude regardless of its direction','A moment that always opposes every joint velocity'],0,'The sign is tied to the selected positive generalized coordinate, not a universal room direction or magnitude.', ['This is the stated convention.','The room’s upward direction need not be the positive joint axis.','Magnitude alone has no directional sign.','Opposition to velocity describes damping, not the definition of positive torque.'],typ='definition',diff='easy')
add(342,'last-link-torque','Why the distal torque depends on f_y','In the example’s frame3 force components, why does τ2=L2 f_y contain no f_x term?', ['The f_x component is parallel to the last-link moment arm, so its cross product is zero','The f_x component is zero for every possible applied force','The second actuator can respond only to force magnitudes','The x component was discarded because it is expressed in the wrong frame'],0,'The offset is L2 along x2, and frames2/3 are aligned; only the transverse y force creates a z moment about joint2.', ['Parallel vectors have zero cross product.','The source allows nonzero f_x.','Torque depends on the moment arm and signed force direction.','Both components are in the consistent example frame.'],typ='diagram')
add(343,'upstream-force','Rotating the force toward the base','For θ2=90° and frame2 force(f_x,f_y)=(3,4) N, what is the force expressed in frame1?', ['(−4,3) N','(4,−3) N','(3,4) N','(−3,−4) N'],0,'Rz(90°) maps(x,y) to(−y,x).', ['This matches the displayed R12.','This uses the inverse rotation.','This ignores θ2.','This applies a180° rotation.'],typ='numerical',steps=['cos90°=0 and sin90°=1.','f1,x=0×3−1×4=−4.','f1,y=1×3+0×4=3.'],formulas=[form('f1=[cosθ2 f_x−sinθ2 f_y, sinθ2 f_x+cosθ2 f_y]',{'f1':'force in frame1','f_x,f_y':'force in frame2 or aligned frame3','θ2':'relative joint angle'},{'f1,f_x,f_y':'N','θ2':'rad'})])
add([342,343,344],'two-link-torques','Computing both static joint torques','For L1=2 m, L2=1 m, θ2=90° and force in frame3(f_x,f_y)=(3,4) N, what are(τ1,τ2)?', ['(10,4) N·m','(4,4) N·m','(−2,4) N·m','(8,6) N·m'],0,'τ1=2×1×3+(1+2×0)×4=10 and τ2=1×4=4.', ['Both terms and joint2’s moment arm are included.','This omits the first-link moment contribution.','This reverses the sign of the f_x contribution.','This does not follow the correct transpose mapping.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Evaluate sinθ2=1 and cosθ2=0.','τ1=2×3+1×4=10.','τ2=1×4=4.'],formulas=[force2r])
add(344,'transpose-not-inverse','Force mapping versus velocity mapping','Which operation maps a desired end force to joint efforts in the shown model?', ['JᵀF','J⁻¹F','JF','(JᵀJ)⁻¹F without specifying dimensions'],0,'Virtual work yields a transpose, not an inverse; the planar example’s torque matrix equals J³ transposed.', ['This is the shown force relation.','The inverse maps suitable Cartesian velocities to joint rates, not forces to efforts.','J maps joint velocities to task velocities.','This expression is not the derived statics map and may not be dimensionally valid.'],typ='conceptual')
add([334,344],'matching-force-frame','Matching a force with its Jacobian','A force is expressed in frame0, but the stored Jacobian is J³; what is a consistent way to compute efforts?', ['Rotate F⁰ into frame3, then compute(J³)ᵀF³','Use(J³)ᵀF⁰ directly because torque is a scalar per joint','Rotate the resulting joint-effort vector as though its entries were Cartesian axes','Multiply F⁰ by J³ without transpose'],0,'The Jacobian’s output coordinates and the force components must refer to the same task basis and point.', ['This makes the power pairing consistent.','Joint efforts are scalar coordinates, but the dot products producing them still require matching Cartesian components.','Joint-coordinate entries are not a Cartesian vector basis.','This uses the wrong dual operation.'],typ='scenario')
add(344,'force-nullspace','A nonzero force can require zero actuator torque','At θ2=0 and f_y=0, what does the ideal planar formula give for nonzero f_x along the straight arm?', ['τ1=τ2=0, without proving unlimited structural load capacity','τ1=L1 f_x and τ2=L2 f_x','τ1=τ2=f_x regardless of length units','No finite force can be represented because J is singular'],0,'The radial force has zero moment about both joint axes in this ideal geometry; structural stresses and load limits remain separate.', ['This is the null space of Jᵀ and includes the necessary physical limit.','Parallel force and lever arms create no z moment.','Force and torque have different units.','The transpose force map exists even when the velocity inverse does not.'],typ='derivation',formulas=[force2r],assumptions=['Ideal rigid planar arm, no gravity, no terminal couple; this statement does not rate the real robot’s joints or structure.'])
add([342,344],'terminal-couple','Adding a terminal moment','If the same planar arm must also exert a pure terminal z couple M, what changes in the torque formulas?', ['Add M to both τ1 and τ2','Add M only to τ2','Multiply both torques by M','Add L1M to τ1 and L2M to τ2'],0,'A pure z couple propagates unchanged through planar z rotations and acts about both revolute axes.', ['The angular-Jacobian row is[1,1].','The upstream joint also transmits the couple.','Efforts add linearly; multiplication has wrong units.','Multiplying a moment by length gives the wrong units.'],typ='derivation',formulas=[form('τ=J_positionᵀ f+[1,1]ᵀM',{'τ':'two joint torques','f':'tip planar force','M':'terminal z moment','J_position':'planar position Jacobian'},{'τ,M':'N·m','f':'N','J_position':'m/rad'},['Terminal couple is an explicit extension of the zero-couple worked example.'])],assumptions=['This is a derived extension: add the specified terminal couple to the lecture’s force-only example.'])
add(345,'work-dot-product','Work of a transverse force','A force is perpendicular to an infinitesimal translational displacement; what is its translational virtual work?', ['Zero, because the dot product is zero','The product of the two magnitudes','A vector perpendicular to both force and displacement','The force magnitude divided by displacement magnitude'],0,'Work uses F·δx=|F||δx|cosφ, not the magnitude product alone.', ['At90°, the directional projection vanishes.','The magnitude product assumes aligned vectors.','That describes a cross-product direction, not scalar work.','That ratio is stiffness-like, not energy.'],typ='conceptual',formulas=[virtual])
add([345,346],'virtual-work-units','Scalar virtual work','Which quantity and units describe F·δx for a pure translational force?', ['Scalar energy in joules','Vector force in newtons','Angular velocity in radians per second','Moment vector in newton-meters'],0,'A force-displacement dot product is scalar work, with dimensions N·m=J.', ['The type and units both match.','A dot product does not return a force vector.','Time does not appear in displacement work.','Although moment shares N·m dimensions, it is a different vector quantity.'],typ='definition',diff='easy')
add(346,'virtual-work-derivation','Why the transpose appears','Which step allows FᵀJδq=τᵀδq to imply τ=JᵀF?', ['The equality holds for every admissible infinitesimal joint displacement δq','J is required to be a square orthogonal matrix','The joint displacement has a unit Euclidean norm','F and τ must have identical numbers of components'],0,'For independent joint coordinates, equality of the linear forms for arbitrary δq identifies their coefficients.', ['This is the coefficient argument.','The transpose relation also works for rectangular J.','No unit-length displacement is required.','Task and joint dimensions can differ.'],typ='derivation',formulas=[virtual],assumptions=['Use independent generalized joint coordinates and the ideal virtual-work model.'])
add(346,'joint-dimension','Generalizing the six-joint notation','For a spatial task with n independent joints, what are the dimensions of F, J and τ, respectively?', ['6×1, 6×n, n×1','n×1, n×6, 6×1','6×1, n×6, n×1','n×1, 6×6, n×1 for every n'],0,'The task wrench has six components, while each joint contributes one generalized effort and one Jacobian column.', ['The multiplication JᵀF has dimensions(n×6)(6×1).','This exchanges task and joint dimensions.','That J shape does not map n joint rates to six task velocities.','A6×6 map assumes six joint coordinates, not arbitrary n.'],typ='derivation',assumptions=['The slide labels joint vectors6×1 for a six-joint case; this question explicitly generalizes to n joints.'],formulas=[virtual])
add(346,'rectangular-force-map','Transpose mapping for a redundant task map','For J=[[1,0,1],[0,2,1]] m/rad and task force F=(3,4) N, what is the three-joint effort vector?', ['(3,8,7) N·m','(3,4,0) N·m','(7,8,3) N·m','The effort vector is undefined because J is not square'],0,'Each joint effort is the dot product of its Jacobian column with F.', ['The three column dot products are3,8 and7.','This ignores the second-column scale and third column.','This interchanges column assignments.','The transpose product does not require a square inverse.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Column1·F=1×3+0×4=3.','Column2·F=0×3+2×4=8.','Column3·F=1×3+1×4=7.'],formulas=[virtual],assumptions=['The stated numerical matrix is a hypothetical local planar task Jacobian; all three generalized coordinates are revolute.'])
add(346,'power-equality','Checking task and joint power','For J=[[1,0,1],[0,2,1]], F=(3,4) N and qdot=(0.2,−0.1,0.3) rad/s, what common power follows?', ['1.9 W','2.7 W','0.7 W','0 W because one joint rate is negative'],0,'v=Jqdot=(0.5,0.1), giving F·v=1.5+0.4=1.9 W; τ=(3,8,7) gives the same joint power.', ['Both power calculations agree.','This ignores the negative contribution of joint2.','This omits one or more transmitted contributions.','Negative rate changes a signed contribution, not necessarily the total to zero.'],typ='numerical',diff='hard',steps=['Compute task velocity(0.5,0.1) m/s.','Compute task power3×0.5+4×0.1=1.9 W.','Compute τ=JᵀF=(3,8,7) N·m.','Compute joint power3×0.2+8×(−0.1)+7×0.3=1.9 W.'],formulas=[virtual,form('P=Fᵀv=τᵀqdot',{'P':'mechanical power','F':'task force','v':'task velocity','τ':'joint torques','qdot':'joint rates'},{'P':'W','F':'N','v':'m/s','τ':'N·m','qdot':'rad/s'})],assumptions=['The numerical J is in m/rad; ideal instantaneous mapping with consistent force convention.'])
add([339,346],'external-force-sign','Applied wrench versus environmental reaction','If F_ext is instead defined as the environment’s wrench on the robot, which no-gravity equilibrium sign convention matches an actuator effort balancing it?', ['τ_act=−JᵀF_ext','τ_act=+JᵀF_ext with no change in the meaning of either symbol','τ_act=J⁻¹F_ext','τ_act=−JF_ext for any matrix shape'],0,'The lecture’s positive applied-hand wrench is opposite to the environment’s reaction; static generalized force balance is τ_act+JᵀF_ext=0.', ['This keeps action/reaction and equilibrium consistent.','Changing which body receives the force requires changing its sign.','Inversion is not the force mapping.','The transpose is required for the joint/task dual.'],typ='derivation',formulas=[form('τ_act+JᵀF_ext=0',{'τ_act':'actuator generalized effort','F_ext':'environmental wrench on the robot','J':'matching task Jacobian'},{'revolute effort':'N·m','prismatic effort':'N','force components':'N','moment components':'N·m'},['No gravity or other generalized loads; static equilibrium.'])])
add(347,'six-vector-order','Order of Cartesian velocity and force components','Which ordering is used for the two six-component vectors on the slide?', ['Velocity:[v;ω]; wrench:[F;N]','Velocity:[ω;v]; wrench:[F;N]','Velocity:[v;ω]; wrench:[N;F]','Velocity:[position;orientation]; wrench:[F;N]'],0,'The lecture places three linear components before three angular components, and force before moment.', ['This gives the power pairing F·v+N·ω.','This reverses the velocity order.','This reverses the wrench order.','The first vector contains rates, not pose coordinates.'],typ='match',diff='easy')
add(347,'fixed-body-transform','When the six-by-six velocity transform applies','Why is the relative joint-rate term crossed out before the fixed frame-to-frame velocity transform?', ['The two frames are treated as fixed on one rigid body, so they have no relative joint motion','A rotation matrix always makes every relative joint rate zero','Angular velocity is omitted from every Cartesian transform','Only translations can be represented by a6×6 matrix'],0,'The displayed transform relates two fixed reference frames on the same rigid body; an articulated joint would add relative motion.', ['This states the needed assumption.','Coordinate rotation does not cancel physical relative motion.','Angular velocity occupies the lower three components.','Both rotation and origin offset appear in the block matrix.'],typ='diagram',assumptions=['Interpret the blue strike on the relative-rate term as the fixed-body special case, not a general moving-joint identity.'])
add(347,'twist-offset-sign','Sign of the velocity offset block','Let p point from origin A to origin B, expressed in A, and R convert A components into B; why is the upper-right block −R[p]×?', ['Because v_B expressed in A is v_A+ω×p=v_A−p×ω','Because v_B=v_A+p×ω by the right-hand rule','Because angular velocity changes sign under every rotation','Because an origin shift reverses all linear velocity components'],0,'Cross-product antisymmetry gives ω×p=−p×ω before the coordinate rotation.', ['This derives the block sign.','This reverses the cross-product order without a sign change.','Proper rotation changes components, not the physical vector sign universally.','Only the rotational offset term is added; translation does not negate all velocity.'],typ='derivation',steps=['Write the velocity at displaced point B as v_A+ω×p.','Replace ω×p by−[p]×ω.','Rotate both terms into B coordinates.'],formulas=[form('v_B^B=R_BA(v_A^A+ω^A×p^A)',{'v_B^B':'B-origin velocity in B coordinates','v_A^A':'A-origin velocity in A coordinates','R_BA':'rotation from A to B coordinates','ω^A':'rigid-body angular velocity in A','p^A':'A-origin to B-origin vector in A'},{'v_B^B,v_A^A':'m/s','ω^A':'rad/s','p^A':'m','R_BA':'dimensionless'})])
add(347,'twist-numerical','Changing both origin and coordinates','Let p_A→B=(1,0,0) m, R_BA=Rz(−90°), v_A^A=(1,0,0) m/s and ω^A=(0,0,2) rad/s; what is v_B^B?', ['(2,−1,0) m/s','(−2,−1,0) m/s','(0,−1,0) m/s','(1,2,0) m/s'],0,'The B-point velocity in A is(1,2,0); rotating by−90° gives(2,−1,0).', ['This includes both origin shift and basis change.','This reverses ω×p.','This omits the offset velocity.','This is the correct vector before rotating its components into B.'],typ='numerical',diff='hard',steps=['ω×p=(0,2,0) m/s.','v_B^A=(1,2,0) m/s.','Rz(−90°)(1,2,0)=(2,−1,0) m/s.'],formulas=[form('v_B^B=R_BA(v_A^A+ω^A×p^A)',{'v_B^B,v_A^A':'origin velocities in the indicated bases','R_BA':'A-to-B rotation','ω^A':'angular velocity','p^A':'A-to-B origin offset'},{'v_B^B,v_A^A':'m/s','ω^A':'rad/s','p^A':'m','R_BA':'dimensionless'})])
add(348,'wrench-offset','Changing the reference point of a wrench','With R_AB converting B components into A and p_A→B expressed in A, which relation is correct?', ['F_A=R_AB F_B; N_A=R_AB N_B+p×F_A','F_A=R_AB F_B+p×N_B; N_A=R_AB N_B','F_A=R_AB F_B; N_A=R_AB N_B−p×F_A','F_A=R_AB F_B; N_A=N_B+p×F_B without rotating those terms'],0,'Changing the moment reference origin adds the moment of the force about A; all terms must share A coordinates.', ['This matches the displayed block matrix.','A shifted origin does not add a moment cross product into force.','The given p direction requires the plus sign.','This mixes B and A components when the bases differ.'],typ='derivation',formulas=[stat])
add(348,'wrench-numerical','A rotated and translated force-moment pair','Let R_AB=Rz(90°), p_A→B=(1,0,0) m, F_B=(2,0,0) N and N_B=(0,0,3) N·m; what are(F_A,N_A)?', ['((0,2,0) N,(0,0,5) N·m)','((0,2,0) N,(0,0,1) N·m)','((2,0,0) N,(0,0,3) N·m)','((0,2,0) N,(0,0,3) N·m)'],0,'Rotate the force to(0,2,0); its moment about A is(0,0,2), which adds to the rotated3 N·m couple.', ['Both operations and signs are included.','This subtracts the offset moment.','This ignores the basis change and offset.','This rotates but omits the origin-shift contribution.'],typ='numerical',diff='hard',steps=['F_A=Rz(90°)(2,0,0)=(0,2,0).','p×F_A=(0,0,2).','R_AB N_B=(0,0,3).','N_A=(0,0,5).'],formulas=[stat])
add([347,348],'power-dual-transform','Duality of velocity and wrench transforms','If ν_B=Xν_A for the shown fixed-body velocity map, what wrench relation preserves power W_Aᵀν_A=W_Bᵀν_B?', ['W_A=XᵀW_B','W_A=XW_B','W_A=X⁻¹W_B','W_A=−XᵀW_B when no force-sign convention changed'],0,'Substituting ν_B=Xν_A into the right-hand power gives W_BᵀXν_A=(XᵀW_B)ᵀν_A.', ['This is the dual map in the opposite reference direction.','Using the same matrix does not generally preserve the dot product.','Inverse alone is not the required transpose dual.','No sign reversal is introduced for the same wrench convention.'],typ='derivation',diff='hard',steps=['Start with W_Bᵀν_B.','Substitute Xν_A for ν_B.','Transpose the coefficient W_BᵀX.','Identify W_A=XᵀW_B.'],formulas=[form('ν_B=Xν_A; W_A=XᵀW_B; P=Wᵀν',{'ν_A,ν_B':'linear-angular velocity vectors about the respective origins','W_A,W_B':'force-moment vectors about the respective origins','X':'fixed-body velocity transform','P':'power'},{'linear velocity':'m/s','angular velocity':'rad/s','force':'N','moment':'N·m','P':'W'})])
add([347,348],'origin-versus-basis','When block-diagonal rotation is sufficient','For fixed frames A and B with the same origin but different orientations, what do the velocity and wrench maps reduce to?', ['Separate rotation of linear/angular and force/moment components with zero offset blocks','A translation-only map with no rotation blocks','A sign reversal of every moment component','A scalar scaling of all six components by the distance between the origins'],0,'When p=0, all cross-product offset terms vanish; the appropriate coordinate rotation remains on both diagonal blocks.', ['This is the p=0 special case.','The orientations still differ.','Changing basis is not a universal sign reversal.','The distance is zero and does not define a scaling operation.'],typ='derivation',formulas=[stat])
add(349,'outward-inward-duality','Velocity and statics propagation','Which summary correctly contrasts the two recursions taught in the lecture?', ['Velocities propagate base-to-tip from joint rates; static loads propagate tip-to-base from the specified end wrench','Both propagate base-to-tip from the same force boundary data','Both propagate tip-to-base from the same joint-rate boundary data','Velocity propagation requires Jᵀ, while force propagation requires J⁻¹'],0,'The kinematic recursion accumulates motion outward; the load recursion accumulates force and moment inward.', ['This preserves both boundary conditions and directions.','The motion and load problems use different inputs.','Joint rates do not serve as a distal wrench boundary.','The direct task relations are v=Jqdot andτ=JᵀF.'],typ='match')
